### Mutation Testing
<!--
Optional ADVANCED OVERRIDE. Tells the agent which mutation-testing tool
(Stryker.NET for .NET, Stryker.JS for TS/JS, or Stryker for Scala/SBT) this
project uses, and when to invoke it. Read by the agent during
/flow, /bug, /build, /review-pr verification phases.

**Most projects don't need this block.** Mutation testing is ON BY DEFAULT
for every ticket that creates unit tests — no PROJECT.md edits required. The
`Mutation testing` field in `/start`'s confirmation summary already defaults
to `Yes — auto-detect tool at verification (recommended)`, and `/flow` Step
11b auto-skips tickets that can't have unit tests (config, docs, infra,
renames). To skip Stryker on one ticket, edit the field to `No` or say
"no stryker" / "skip stryker" in your invocation.

This block exists only to DEVIATE from the default-on behaviour:
- `when_to_run: before_pr` — same as the default, but PINS a specific tool
  (skips auto-detection) — useful when auto-detect would be ambiguous.
- `when_to_run: test_only_prs` — default-on only for test-only PRs.
- `when_to_run: manual` — opt the project OUT of default-on; Stryker then
  runs only when explicitly requested per ticket. Use this for repos where
  the suite is too slow to mutate on every ticket.

**Setting up the block:**

- **Easy path (recommended):** run `/init` and pick `Yes` when asked about
  mutation testing. The toolbox writes this block + generates the minimal
config file for you (`stryker-config.json` / `stryker.config.mjs`). See
`commands/init.md` Step 5 sub-step 1c.
- **Manual path:** edit this block directly. Update `tool`, `when_to_run`,
  `config_path`, `output_dir` to match your project. Then create the
  matching config file at the project root yourself.

**To remove this override:** delete this entire section. The project
reverts to the global default-on behaviour — Stryker runs by default on
tickets that create unit tests, no project config required. (To turn
mutation testing OFF for the project instead, keep the block and set
`when_to_run: manual`.)

⛔ THE BLOCK CONTAINS NO SHELL COMMANDS. PROJECT.md is committed to the
repo and editable via PR. If we let projects supply `run_command` / etc.
here, a malicious PR could replace `dotnet stryker` with arbitrary shell
that the agent then executes during verification. Instead: the agent
reads `tool` from this block and looks up the actual install + run
commands from a toolbox-owned registry in the `workflow-discipline`
skill (§1a) — a toolbox-internal file that PRs cannot modify without
going through the toolbox's own code-review-orchestration first.
Automated review caught this attack path on PR #58.

The toolbox already has a guardrail in `rules/testing.md` ("Stryker
Mutation Testing — Production Code Boundary") preventing the agent from
modifying production code to kill surviving mutants. This block tells
the agent WHICH tool and WHEN; the rule + registry tells it HOW.
-->

```yaml
mutation_testing:
  # Required. Which tool runs. Must be in the toolbox registry
  # (rules/testing.md) — currently: stryker.net | stryker.js | scala-sbt.
  # Unsupported tool name → agent refuses to autonomously invoke;
  # user must run the tool manually.
  tool: "stryker.net"
  # Required. Path to the tool's config file, relative to repo root.
  # This IS user-controlled but it's a file path, not shell.
  config_path: "src/MyProject.Tests/stryker-config.json"
  # When to invoke:
  #   - "test_only_prs" — runs on PRs that touch only test files (default
  #     for test-coverage-focused work)
  #   - "manual" — user must explicitly ask ("run stryker", "check
  #     mutation score") — RECOMMENDED for projects with non-default
  #     tool wrappers or unusual setups
  #   - "before_pr" — runs on every PR verification (heavy; not
  #     recommended for fast-moving feature work)
  when_to_run: "test_only_prs"
  # Output directory — gitignored by /init. Stryker.NET defaults to
  # StrykerOutput/; Stryker.JS defaults to reports/mutation/. Scala/SBT
  # output is project-configured; add its path to .gitignore if needed.
  output_dir: "StrykerOutput"
  # Guardrail reference — surviving mutants are REPORTED as findings,
  # NEVER an excuse to modify production code. See rules/testing.md.
  guardrail: "rules/testing.md — Stryker Mutation Testing — Production Code Boundary"
```

### Verification

<!--
Optional ADVANCED OVERRIDE. Declares this project's definition of done in a
form the agent can read without guessing: which ecosystem's commands to use,
which gates count, what coverage bar to hold, and which paths are tests.

**Most projects don't need this block.** With no block the agent detects the
ecosystem by which manifest FILE exists, runs every gate that ecosystem has a
command for, and holds the toolbox's own 80% coverage floor. An absent block is
fully functional — this block exists ONLY to DEVIATE:

- pin `runner:` when auto-detection would be ambiguous (mixed monorepo);
- narrow `gates:` when one gate is genuinely not part of "done" here;
- RAISE `coverage_floor:` above the toolbox minimum;
- correct `test_globs:` when the project's test paths are unusual.

**To remove this override:** delete this entire section. The project reverts to
auto-detection plus the toolbox defaults.

⛔ THE BLOCK CONTAINS NO SHELL COMMANDS. Same trust boundary as
`mutation_testing` above, for the same reason: PROJECT.md is committed and
editable through a PR, so any command STRING here would be an unreviewed
code-execution path into the agent's environment. There is deliberately no
`test_command`, `lint_command`, `build_command`, or `smoke_command` field, and
adding one is not a shortcut — it is the attack path.

The agent reads the KEYS below and looks the literal commands up in the
toolbox-owned verification registry in the `workflow-discipline` skill (§1c) —
a file a project PR cannot modify without going through the toolbox's own
review first. Automated review caught this attack path on PR #58 (see the
`mutation_testing` note above); `knowledge/toolbox-lessons.yml` TL-0005 records
the general rule: detect which manifest FILE EXISTS, then run a fixed command
for that ecosystem — the file's presence is the signal, its contents are never
a command.

Unrecognised values fail CLOSED: the agent refuses to run that gate and asks
you to run it manually, rather than guessing a command.

**Why there is no `smoke_command`.** A smoke check is inherently
project-specific — start this service, hit this endpoint, expect this string.
That makes it neither a fixed registry entry (there is no generic string to
register) nor a PROJECT.md field (that is the forbidden shape). The registry
does not register a generic smoke command. A project that needs a smoke check
runs it manually and tells the agent the result.
-->

```yaml
verification:
  # Optional. Which ecosystem's fixed commands to use. `auto` (the default,
  # and the behaviour when this whole block is absent) detects by which
  # manifest FILE EXISTS — never by reading a file's contents.
  # Valid values are exactly the registry keys in the `workflow-discipline`
  # skill §1c: auto | dotnet | maven-wrapper | maven | gradle-wrapper |
  # gradle | node-pnpm | node-yarn | node-npm | python.
  # An unrecognised value fails closed — nothing runs and the agent asks.
  runner: auto
  # Optional. Which gates make up "done" here. ACTION NAMES ONLY, restricted
  # to the three the registry knows: test | lint | build. Any other value is
  # refused for that entry. Omit the key to run every gate the resolved
  # runner has a command for. A gate the runner has no registry command for
  # is reported UNAVAILABLE — never silently counted as passed.
  gates: [test, lint, build]
  # Optional. Minimum test coverage percentage, as an integer.
  # ⛔ RAISE-ONLY CLAMP: the effective floor is max(80, this value). The 80%
  # minimum is already asserted in the always-loaded `rules/testing.md` and
  # its provider adapter, so a project may hold itself to a HIGHER bar but can
  # never lower the toolbox-wide guarantee — a PR setting `coverage_floor: 10`
  # would otherwise retire that guarantee with nothing in the diff that looks
  # like a policy change. A value below 80 is kept in the file and ignored at
  # runtime (failing the run instead would let one bad line block every
  # workflow).
  coverage_floor: 85
  # Optional. Path patterns that count as TEST files. Used for the test-only
  # PR boundary (`rules/workflow-discipline.md` Rule 6 — a test-only PR must
  # not touch production code). These are PATHS matched against changed
  # files: never executed, never passed to a shell, never shell-expanded.
  # ⛔ NARROW-ONLY CLAMP: a pattern is IGNORED at runtime unless it names a
  # test path SEGMENT — not merely a substring. Lower-case the pattern and
  # look for `test`, `tests`, `spec`, `specs`, `e2e`, `fixture` or
  # `fixtures`; the pattern is kept only if one of those occurrences has a
  # delimiter on BOTH sides, where a delimiter is the start/end of the
  # pattern or one of exactly FIVE characters: `/` `.` `_` `-` `,`. NOTHING
  # ELSE DELIMITS — not a letter or digit, not a Unicode letter (so
  # `caféspec/**` and `Ωtest/**` are discarded, not kept), and not `*` or `?`.
  # A WILDCARD NEVER DELIMITS, on either side: `*` and `?`
  # absorb arbitrary characters including letters, which is exactly what
  # defeats a delimiter test. So `**/*test/**` would match `latest/` and
  # `**/*test*/**` would match `app/attestation/` — both are ignored, and so
  # is the .NET `*Tests` suffix shape (`**/*Tests.cs` matches
  # `src/LatestTests.cs`; `**/*Tests/**` matches `src/LatestTests/`). Patterns
  # containing `..`, brace alternation (`{`/`}`), a character class
  # (`[`/`]`), extglob (`(`, `)`, `|`) or a LEADING `!` are ignored outright,
  # before the delimiter test: each smuggles in a match the delimiter test
  # cannot see — `{tests,src}/**` widens to `src`; `src/[test]/**` accepts
  # `src/t/**` because `[test]` matches ONE character, not the word;
  # `!(tests)/**` expands to every top-level entry EXCEPT tests; and
  # `!tests/**` is negation, so it means every path NOT under tests. That
  # last one is the easy accident — if you meant "exclude tests", this key is
  # the wrong place to say it: `test_globs` lists what IS a test.
  # KEPT:    `tests/**`, `__tests__/**`, `**/Tests/**`, `**/*.Tests/**`,
  #          `**/*.Tests.cs`, `**/*.spec.ts`, `*.test.*`, `**/*_test.py`
  # IGNORED: `**/*Tests/**`, `**/*Tests.cs`, `*test/**`, `**/*test*/**`,
  #          `src/[test]/**`, `latest/**`, `contest/**`, `attestation/**`,
  #          `src/inspector/**`, `app/Specifications/**`, `src/respec/**`,
  #          and the catch-alls `**`, `**/*`, `*`
  # Rule 6 cannot be emptied from here. Same reason `coverage_floor` above
  # is raise-only: this file is outside the toolbox trust boundary, so it
  # must not be able to retire a toolbox-wide guarantee with a line that
  # does not look like a policy change.
  # TWO REAL COSTS, both worth knowing before you write a pattern:
  #  1. A legitimate test path that names none of those seven literals
  #     (`unit/**`, `testing/**`, `TestUtils/**`, Perl's `t/**`) is ignored.
  #     The only loosening that would admit `testing/**` or `TestUtils/**`
  #     is letting a letter follow the keyword, and that also re-admits
  #     `app/Specifications/**` and `TESTAMENT/**`; `unit/**` and `t/**`
  #     contain no literal at all.
  #  2. The `*Tests` suffix convention is common in .NET and is ignored,
  #     because it is the same shape as the laundering it enables (see
  #     `src/LatestTests.cs` above). Use `**/Tests/**` or `**/*.Tests/**` for
  #     test directories, and `**/*.Tests.cs` for a file carrying a literal
  #     `.Tests.`. Nothing covers a bare `CustomerServiceTests.cs` by
  #     filename — in the standard .NET layout it lives inside a test
  #     project directory, so a directory glob covers it instead.
  # Nothing is dropped silently: every ignored pattern is reported BY NAME,
  # with the clause that ignored it, and those files fall back to the
  # agent's own judgement of which paths are tests — extra scrutiny, never
  # less. Exact rule: the `workflow-discipline` skill §1c, "How the agent
  # uses the block", step 5.
  test_globs:
    - "tests/**"
    - "**/*.test.ts"
    - "**/Tests/**"
```

### Loop Budget

<!--
Optional ADVANCED OVERRIDE. Bounds unattended iteration, in TWO places:

- ONE self-paced `/loop` run — the loop body itself lives in `.claude/loop.md`
  (template: `examples/loop.md.example`); and
- the `/goal` exit conditions `/flow` sets inside its Implementation and
  Verification phases, which are written from the clamped `max_turns` and
  `no_progress_after` (see `commands/flow.md` → "`/goal` — Machine-Checkable
  Phase Exits").

That second consumer matters when you size these: an interactive `/goal` was
never launched with a spend flag, so `max_spend_usd` does NOT bound it and the
other two fields are its entire budget.

**Most projects don't need this block.** With no block the toolbox defaults
apply (see the `workflow-discipline` skill §1d). An absent block is fully
functional.

⛔ TIGHTEN-ONLY CLAMP. The effective value of every field is
min(project value, toolbox ceiling). The ceilings live in the toolbox
(`workflow-discipline` skill §1d) and are deliberately NOT settable here, for
the same reason `rules/performance.md` keeps model and effort out of
PROJECT.md: a behaviour-critical knob must not be raisable by a file that every
contributor can edit through a PR. These three bound how long an agent runs
unattended and how much it spends. A project may make its loop MORE cautious;
it can never buy itself more turns, more idle iterations, or more money. A
value above the ceiling is kept in the file and clamped at runtime.

Every field is a NUMBER compared against a counter. None is a command, and
none is interpolated into one. `max_turns` and `max_spend_usd` have NATIVE
harness primitives (`CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_TURNS`, `--max-budget-usd`) — the
supported `scripts/loop-launch.sh` applies them, and the toolbox deliberately
keeps no turn counter or spend ledger of its own, because a second source of
truth drifts.
`no_progress_after` has no native primitive: it is compared by
`~/.claude/scripts/loop-progress-signature.sh`, which the loop body has to call
(see the `loop-design` skill, guard 2).

The supported `scripts/loop-launch.sh` launcher reads these numeric values and
applies the toolbox ceilings before invoking Claude's native turn and spend
controls. It also exports the no-progress bound for loop bodies. Use that
launcher for a project-configured loop; direct `/loop` and `/goal` invocations
still require their own declared condition clauses and hook checks.
-->

```yaml
loop:
  # Iteration ceiling for one loop run. Clamped to the toolbox ceiling. The
  # native CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_TURNS is applied by scripts/loop-launch.sh.
  max_turns: 20
  # Stop and escalate after this many consecutive iterations that changed
  # nothing. This is the "am I actually getting anywhere" guard — a loop that
  # keeps re-reading the same green PR should quit, not keep paying.
  no_progress_after: 2
  # Spend cap in USD for one loop run. The launcher applies it as
  # --max-budget-usd.
  max_spend_usd: 1.50
```

<!--
DEPRECATED: jira_columns

Older /init runs saved the full ordered column list to a top-level
`jira_columns` field. The toolbox now reasons in PHASES, not column ordinals,
so the column list is no longer the source of truth — `jira_phase_map` is.

Migration: /flow Step 0 detects old PROJECT.md files that still have
`jira_columns` but no `jira_phase_map` and runs the anchor questions once to
populate the map. The legacy field is then removed automatically.

If you see `jira_columns: ...` in your PROJECT.md, leave it for now — the
next /flow run will migrate it.
-->
